;; What fraction of what AIUEOS CLAIMS does a real boot actually prove, and how
;; does the shape of that claim compare to the two operating systems worth
;; comparing against?
;;
;; The measured column is deliberately NOT source presence. The tree declares
;; 131 distinct AIUEOS_*_OK markers; a release boot emits far fewer. That gap
;; is the whole point: a marker in a .c file is an intention, a marker in a
;; serial log is a proof. Only the second counts here.
;;
;; TWO BENCHMARKS, AND THEY ARE NOT THE SAME QUESTION.
;;
;; Linux is the wrong yardstick for almost every row and is kept only as
;; context. It is a general-purpose kernel with a driver matrix no scoped OS
;; will ever match, and "Linux has it" is true of nearly everything, so it
;; discriminates nothing. Chasing it produces a plan measured in person-decades
;; and tells you nothing about whether the node works.
;;
;; ChromeOS is the right yardstick. It is an appliance OS: verified boot, A/B
;; slots with automatic rollback, a minimal userland, no general package
;; management, and a fixed hardware set per device. That is the same shape
;; AIUEOS is aiming at, so the comparison is actionable -- and in several rows
;; AIUEOS is already closer to ChromeOS than to Linux, because signed object
;; admission and a dual-slot journal ARE verified boot and A/B updates under
;; different names.
;;
;; :unmeasured is not zero (the property is borrowed from
;; kotoba-lang/catalog-maturity). A subsystem no evidence log covers is
;; excluded from the total and reported in :measured-weight, so silence is
;; never scored as failure -- and the report refuses to produce a number at
;; all when given no evidence.
{:format :aiueos.os-coverage/v1
 :measured-by "os/aiueos/scripts/os-coverage.cljk"
 :evidence-is :emitted-markers-in-a-real-boot-log
 :not-evidence :markers-present-in-source

 ;; :relevance says whether a row is on the path to the stated target, which is
 ;; an appliance inference node -- not a workstation. Rows marked :context are
 ;; measured and reported but excluded from the headline number, because
 ;; scoring an inference node on desktop compositing would make the number
 ;; move for reasons nobody acts on.
 ;; Some markers are emitted with an _OK suffix in BOTH the state they name and
 ;; its opposite, so their NAME is not proof. AIUEOS_DMA_POLICY_OK is the
 ;; measured case: under VT-d it reads `dmar=validated dma=vtd-isolated`, and on
 ;; a machine with no DMAR it reads `dmar=absent test-only-unisolated` -- the
 ;; same marker announcing that isolation is absent. Counting the name credited
 ;; an explicitly unisolated boot as a proven capability.
 ;;
 ;; A marker listed here counts only when its line also contains the substring.
 ;; Keep this small: it is for markers whose _OK is a report rather than a
 ;; verdict, not a place to encode every payload.
 ;; "validated", not "isolated". The first attempt used "isolated" and did not
 ;; discriminate at all, because `test-only-unisolated` contains it -- the
 ;; negative case is spelled as the positive with a prefix. Caught by running
 ;; the control instead of trusting the substring.
 :requires-payload {:DMA_POLICY "validated"}

 ;; WHY a marker is unproven, where it is known. Without this the report says
 ;; thirty markers are unproven and treats them alike, when they need five
 ;; different things and only one of them is code someone could sit down and
 ;; write. That flattening is the exact error this file warns about in its
 ;; header: a zero that means "this profile did not exercise it" read as a zero
 ;; that means "it does not work".
 ;;
 ;; A marker absent from this map is unclassified, which is not the same as
 ;; unblocked -- the report says so rather than implying a lever exists.
 :unproven-because
 {;; needs the AIUEOS guest running on the physical K16; QEMU cannot present
  ;; the device or table (MEASURED 2026-09-09: QEMU amd-iommu attaches on q35
  ;; but no IVRS reaches the guest through OVMF -- AIUEOS_IVRS_ABSENT).
  ;; MEASURED 2026-11-03 on the powered board itself (aiueos-node-260910,
  ;; Ryzen 7 7735HS, up 53d): firmware DOES publish IVRS at power-on
  ;; (/sys/firmware/acpi/tables/IVRS, 420 B, sha256 5e692e8e2cd2..., DMAR
  ;; count 0) and the host kernel's AMD-Vi parses it (/sys/class/iommu/ivhd0).
  ;; The table the guest would read exists on the board; what remains
  ;; unmeasured is the guest's own IVRS path on that silicon. Bucket
  ;; unchanged (:board).
  :IVRS :board  :PHYSICAL_NETWORK :board  :RTL8125 :board
  :PHYSICAL_DIRECT_HTTPS :board  :PHYSICAL_QUALIFICATION :board
  :PHYSICAL_QWEN35 :board  :PHYSICAL_MODEL_HANDOFF :board
  ;; needs a firmware this machine does not have (TPM-enabled OVMF).
  ;; MEASURED 2026-09-10, not assumed: swtpm and swtpm_setup ARE installed and
  ;; QEMU has the emulator backend, so the device side is available. With a
  ;; real software TPM attached the guest still reports
  ;;   AIUEOS_TPM2_ABSENT no-root-of-trust-described
  ;; so the blocker is OVMF declining to publish a TPM2 ACPI table, not the
  ;; absence of a TPM. MEASURED 2026-11-03 on the powered K16: its firmware
  ;; DOES publish a TPM2 ACPI table (/sys/firmware/acpi/tables/TPM2, 76 B,
  ;; sha256 e79c1876f9aa...), so the board route is not firmware-blocked --
  ;; but the guest's own TPM2 path on the board is unexercised and the QEMU
  ;; route stays blocked exactly as measured, so the bucket is unchanged
  ;; (:firmware). Re-test with:
  ;;   AIUEOS_TEST_TPM2=1 sh os/aiueos/scripts/smoke-qemu-uefi.sh
  :TPM2 :firmware
  ;; blocked in the compiler, not in aiueos. MEASURED 2026-09-10 against THREE
  ;; amu revisions -- the shared checkout (72e67e5e), the west pin (fac4176c),
  ;; and origin/main (2abd9843, fetched, pin is its ancestor, +2). Identical
  ;; exit 70 :kotoba/internal-error at all three, so ADVANCING THE PIN DOES NOT
  ;; FIX THIS and that hypothesis need not be retried:
  ;; cap-call crashes the native backend even though the PLC policy grants it.
  ;;   echo '(ns p (:export [main])) (defn main [] (cap-call 16 0))' > /tmp/p.kotoba
  ;;   <amu>/bin/kotoba-compiler compile /tmp/p.kotoba \
  ;;     --target x86_64-aiueos-user-v1 \
  ;;     --policy os/aiueos/kotoba/plc-runtime-policy.edn --output /tmp/p.elf
  ;;   -> exit 70, :kotoba/internal-error
  ;; The target itself is fine: (defn main [] 0) compiles to 8560 bytes, and so
  ;; do the generated plc-dint helper and its 2^32 arithmetic. Only cap-call
  ;; fails, and plc-runtime-policy.edn allows [:cap/call 16..19] explicitly, so
  ;; this is not a refusal wearing the wrong message -- a permitted form is
  ;; crashing the backend. Nor is it a missing lowering: cap-call is present in
  ;; amu (capability_names, backend/cljs, elaboration-pipeline "scalar cap-call
  ;; path first") and in kotoba-native (machine_ir, elf64, peephole, aarch64).
  ;; DIAGNOSED 2026-09-10. The real error is not "internal compiler error" --
  ;; that string is an artifact of the reporting. amu's nbb CLI
  ;; (compiler/nbb/cli_support.cljs `error-report`) reads (ex-data error) and,
  ;; finding no :phase, defaults to :internal and DISCARDS the message. The
  ;; underlying exception is a plain JS TypeError with nil ex-data:
  ;;   TypeError: Cannot mix BigInt and other types, use explicit conversions
  ;; i.e. a Number/BigInt mix on the nbb path, where i64 literals are BigInt.
  ;; Bisected by phase, so the search space is small:
  ;;   amu check <src> --policy <p>            -> :ok true (frontend, link,
  ;;                                              typecheck, admission all fine)
  ;;   amu compile --target js                 -> :ok true, 58076 bytes
  ;;   amu compile --target x86_64-aiueos-user-v1 -> exit 70, the TypeError
  ;; So it is the x86_64 NATIVE EMISSION only. Trigger is any cap-call at all:
  ;;   (cap-call 16 0) / (cap-call 17 0) / (let [x (cap-call 16 0)] 0) all fail;
  ;;   (defn main [] 0) compiles. Independent of which id and of nesting.
  ;; NOT the obvious suspect: kotoba-native x86_64.cljc `emit-cap-call` already
  ;; coerces the id once up front -- `cap-id #?(:clj cap-id :cljs (js/Number
  ;; cap-id))` -- with a comment naming this exact TypeError, and the copy amu
  ;; actually pins (kotoba-native edbbe987) carries that coercion too. The mix
  ;; is somewhere else on that emission path.
  ;; NARROWED FURTHER 2026-09-10 by instrumenting a throwaway amu worktree.
  ;; Everything below is an ELIMINATION, so the next person does not re-walk it:
  ;;   - x86_64 `emit-cap-call` is NEVER REACHED (probe never fires) and its
  ;;     cap-id is already coerced with (js/Number cap-id) in the pinned copy.
  ;;   - machine_ir `capability-id` already coerces via i64/bigint-value?.
  ;;   - GMIR cap-call lowering IS reached (4x) and does not throw.
  ;;   - `lower-kir-module` does not throw, checked with the lazy seq forced
  ;;     inside the try -- the first probe here was INCONCLUSIVE because the
  ;;     structure is lazy and the exception realized outside the catch.
  ;;   - `entry-fuel-prefixes` does not throw; nor does `compile-kir-module`.
  ;;     So `emit-program` COMPLETES. The fault is not in code generation.
  ;; What is left is the post-emission path: kexe/ELF packaging, provenance,
  ;; the manifest writers, or the artifact digest -- which is where this
  ;; codebase already documents BigInt sensitivity ("EDN has no i64 type
  ;; marker: the writer prints an nbb bigint as the same plain integer token").
  ;; The cap-call trigger is consistent with that: a program containing one is
  ;; the only shape whose artifact carries a capability effect row to serialize.
  ;; The interpreted stack is useless for locating it -- every frame is inside
  ;; nbb_core.js, because SCI-interpreted code carries no source frames. Bisect
  ;; by wrapping stages, and force lazy seqs inside the try.
  ;;
  ;; That elimination was right, and it stopped one step short of the
  ;; function. What follows names it.
  ;;
  ;; FOUND AND FIXED 2026-09-10, later the same day, and PLC_RT IS NOW EMITTED.
  ;; It was not on the emission path. It was in PACKAGING:
  ;; kotoba-native `elf64.cljc`'s `capability-bitmap`, which is called from
  ;; exactly one place -- `package-user`, the aiueos CPL3 image route -- and
  ;; did `(quot id 8)` / `(mod id 8)` on an id that is a bigint on cljs.
  ;;
  ;; The bisect above stopped one target too early, and the missing control is
  ;; the one that names the file: the SAME source compiles on wasm32,
  ;; x86_64-linux, aarch64-macos, x86_64-aiueos-kernel-v1, aarch64-aiueos-
  ;; kernel-v1 and x86_64-aiueos-uefi-v1, and fails ONLY on
  ;; x86_64-aiueos-user-v1 -- while that target compiles fine without cap-call.
  ;; "x86_64 native emission" was too wide by five targets, and the width is
  ;; what sent the reader to x86_64.cljc, where the coercion was already right.
  ;; A failing case tells you what broke; only a passing neighbour tells you
  ;; where.
  ;;
  ;; Fixed in kotoba-native b88a11b (main 5f2717c) with a regression test that
  ;; fails without it -- `packs-a-user-image` could not have caught this: its
  ;; artifact declares no effects, so the reduce ran zero times and a user
  ;; image with NO capabilities was proving the capability path. west pin
  ;; advanced 50c6480 -> 5f2717c (root eb5006c2). amu's deps.edn pins
  ;; kotoba-native by :git/sha, so the fix does not arrive until that advances:
  ;; kotoba-lang/amu#921, edbbe987 -> 5f2717c.
  ;;
  ;; Measured end to end with the advanced pin: the three-line repro exits 0
  ;; with an 8560-byte image; ids 16/17/18 give bitmap bytes 1/2/4; id 20 is
  ;; still refused :kotoba/admission-denied against a policy granting 16..19;
  ;; build-plc-native-program.sh reaches AIUEOS_PLC_NATIVE_BUILD_OK with both
  ;; the build and its reproduction at 8560 bytes; and the kernel prints
  ;; AIUEOS_PLC_RT_OK ... capabilities=16,17,18,19 -- realtime 1/1.
  ;;
  ;; `error-report` discarding the message of an exception with no :phase is
  ;; still true and still worth fixing upstream: it cost a day here, and the
  ;; whole diagnosis above is what one uncaught `(.-message e)` would have
  ;; printed. Left as a separate upstream item, not as a blocker.
  ;;
  ;; The second gap is fixed too: smoke-qemu-uefi.sh defaulted AIUEOS_PLC_ELF
  ;; to build/plc-motor/, a directory nothing in the tree writes, so the
  ;; documented profile could not run unaided. It now defaults to
  ;; build/aiueos-plc/motor/, where build-plc-native-program.sh puts it.
  ;;
  ;; WHAT IS NOT SETTLED: the gate is INTERMITTENT. Seven runs on 2026-09-10
  ;; with the fixed toolchain gave 3 green and 4
  ;; `AIUEOS_PLC_RT_FAIL embedded-elf-provider-or-safe-state loader=7` with
  ;; stage= 92, 25, 4 and 19 -- a different stage each time, which is the shape
  ;; of a timing failure rather than a wrong path (a wrong path fails
  ;; identically). Host load1 was 26-42 with a second QEMU running throughout,
  ;; and the profile it asserts is `release=apic-absolute-ticks cycle=10ticks`
  ;; with `deadline` among its failure modes, so a loaded host is the first
  ;; hypothesis and it has NOT been tested on a quiet one. Do not read the
  ;; green as "the gate is reliable"; read it as "the marker is reachable and
  ;; has been emitted". Measure it again on an idle machine before deciding
  ;; whether the flakiness is the gate's or the host's.
  ;; CORRECTED 2026-09-10, same day. This was recorded as :code -- "a real gap
  ;; in aiueos itself" -- on the strength of the kernel's own failure branch,
  ;; "leftover=one-scanout enabled=1", appearing 36x and 7x in two logs while
  ;; AIUEOS_GUEST_GPU_TWO_OK passed in the same boots under max_outputs=2. The
  ;; evidence was real and the inference was not checked: nobody asked whether a
  ;; profile existed that changes the INPUT to that branch. One does.
  ;; QEMU sets enabled_output_bitmask=1 at realize and a head becomes enabled
  ;; only when a UI frontend calls ui_info with a non-zero size
  ;; (hw/display/virtio-gpu-base.c). `-display none` never does, so under the
  ;; default backend GET_DISPLAY_INFO CANNOT report two scanouts however correct
  ;; the guest is -- and the guest declining to drive a head the host calls
  ;; disabled is right, not a gap. AIUEOS_GUEST_SCANOUT_TWO=1 switches to the
  ;; dbus backend and enables head 1 via gdbus SetUIInfo on Console_1; it needs
  ;; dbus-daemon (brew install dbus). Run that way the guest emits
  ;; AIUEOS_GUEST_SCANOUT_TWO_OK scanouts=2 resource-0=1 resource-1=2 kotoba-n=2
  ;; and display reaches 16/16.
  ;; Kept as a record: a failure branch tells you what happened, not why, and
  ;; reading one as a gap without checking the profile is the error this file's
  ;; own header warns about.
  :GUEST_SCANOUT_TWO :profile
  ;; CORRECTED 2026-09-10. These five were recorded as :owner -- blocked on
  ;; MURAKUMO_SERVICE_TOKEN. The token was never the binding constraint, and two
  ;; measurements say so.
  ;; (1) The secrets map records, re-measured 2026-09-08, that the node plane no
  ;;     longer needs that token at all: api.murakumo.cloud accepts
  ;;     `Authorization: CACAO <base64>` on every relay route, signed by
  ;;     AIUEOS_K16_MURAKUMO_NODE_KEY, so a node needs no shared operator secret.
  ;; (2) kernel/main.c emits AIUEOS_MURAKUMO_NODE_OK only under
  ;;     `if (direct_https_ok)` and prints aiueos_rtl8125_direct_device_did() --
  ;;     a real HTTPS session over the PHYSICAL RTL8125. QEMU reports
  ;;     rtl8125-no-device-bounded; there is no such NIC there.
  ;; So the wall is the board, not a credential. Recording them as :owner
  ;; implied a lever that does not exist -- someone could have gone looking for
  ;; a token that would not have helped.
  ;; What DOES still need the owner is the operator plane: trust-tier
  ;; `awai-secure` requires operator authorization and CACAO cannot reach it
  ;; (401), so K16's correct tier is `community`.
  :MURAKUMO_NODE :board  :MURAKUMO_HEARTBEAT :board  :MURAKUMO_JOB_POLL :board
  :MURAKUMO_JOB_RESULT :board  :MURAKUMO_CONTROL_ACK :board
  :MURAKUMO_PERSISTENT_BOOT :owner  :PHYSICAL_JOB :owner
  :PHYSICAL_LIVENESS :owner  :PHYSICAL_RELAY :owner
