  ;; Added 2026-09-10 by comparison against a running Ubuntu Server (gad,
  ;; 24.04.2): that host exposes rtc, ptp and pps device classes and runs
  ;; systemd-timesyncd, and this taxonomy had no row for a clock at all. The
  ;; capability is not absent from AIUEOS -- uefi/main.c computes
  ;; `info.tsc_hz = elapsed * 10ULL` over a stall and passes it in boot info,
  ;; main.c re-exports it as AIUEOS_DWC_TSC_HZ and aiueos_wait_seconds()
  ;; converts cycles to seconds with it. What is absent is any way to SCORE it:
  ;; `markers-in` only recognises /AIUEOS_[A-Z0-9_]+_OK/, and the calibration
  ;; emits no such marker, so no evidence log can carry it and no row could
  ;; count it.
  ;;
  ;; :TSC_CALIBRATED was a PHANTOM when this row was added -- the taxonomy
  ;; naming a marker the tree did not have. It is real as of 2026-09-10:
  ;; kernel/main.c emits AIUEOS_TSC_CALIBRATED_OK with the frequency, right
  ;; after AIUEOS_SERIAL_OK, and only when the loader actually calibrated one.
  ;; So this row now scores like any other, and a boot that does not calibrate
  ;; reports 0/1 rather than being invisible.
  ;;
  ;; :APIC_TIMER is deliberately NOT repeated here; it is scored under
  ;; :interrupts, and a marker counted in two subsystems would be counted twice.
  :time        {:relevance :core
                :markers [:TSC_CALIBRATED]
                :linux "RTC, clocksource selection with TSC-vs-HPET arbitration,
                        NTP via systemd-timesyncd, PTP and PPS device classes"
                :chromeos "same"
                :note "AIUEOS calibrates the TSC in the loader and carries the
                       frequency in boot info, but announces nothing, so every
                       timing claim downstream has to say
                       `timing=logical-unqualified` (the PLC RT marker does).
                       This row exists to keep that visible until a marker
                       exists."}
